.TH MODMAN-OPEN 1 "2026-06-01" "modman" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modman-open \- GTK opener for one local PFS module file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modman-open FILE
.PP
.B modman-open
[\fB--help\fR|\fB--version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B modman-open
opens exactly one local
.I .pfs
module file from a command line or file manager. It is a small attach and
unload helper, not a full module manager.
.PP
The utility validates the selected file through the
.B modman
backend, shows the module status, size and filesystem details in a GTK dialog,
and offers only the actions that fit that file. It does not show repository
tabs, system module lists, package extraction, package install checkboxes,
safe mode, upper layer options, or batch file handling.
.PP
All runtime changes go through
.B modman.
The GTK utility does not call pfs runtime helpers directly. Attach operations
are sent to the backend with the selected file path. Unload operations use the
module name derived by the backend from that file, then ask
.B modman
to unload that name.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B FILE
Path to one local
.I .pfs
file. The desktop integration passes one file with
.B %f,
not a multi-file
.B %F
argument. Missing files, non-local paths, and wrong extensions are rejected
before attach or unload actions are offered. If the filesystem format cannot be
identified, the dialog shows it as unknown instead of treating that alone as a
hard error.
.SH FILE MANAGER INTEGRATION
.B modman-open.desktop
registers the utility as an open-with handler for
.BR application/x-pfs .
Shared MIME data maps
.I *.pfs
files to the same
.B application/x-pfs
type. After the desktop database and MIME cache are updated by packaging or the
user session, a file manager can offer
.B modman-open
for a single selected PFS module file.
.PP
The desktop entry must use:
.PP
.B Exec=modman-open %f
.PP
This keeps the helper in single-file mode and avoids unsupported batch attach
or unload flows.
.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.B Inspect
The helper asks
.B modman
for machine-readable information about
.I FILE,
including the resolved path, derived module name, format, size, and loaded
state. When helper tools are installed, format details may include
.B unsquashfs
or
.B dump.erofs
metadata such as compression.
.TP
.B Attach
When the file is valid and not already attached, the dialog can attach it
through
.B modman
using the selected local path. The dialog can pass optional lower-layer and RAM
copy flags to the backend; it still does not call pfs runtime helpers directly.
.TP
.B Unload
When the file appears attached, the dialog can unload it through
.B modman
using the derived module name. If the backend reports that the module is busy,
blocked by dependencies, or otherwise unsafe to remove, the dialog reports that
failure and leaves the module attached.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Help, version, or completed dialog flow.
.TP
.B 1
Missing required file argument or command-line usage error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MODMAN_BIN
Optional backend path used by tests and custom environments. If unset, the
utility uses the configured backend default.
.SH SEE ALSO
modman(8), modman-tui(8), modman.conf(5), update-desktop-database(1),
update-mime-database(1)
